  "meta": {
    "title": "Why Russia Owns a Piece of the EU 🇷🇺🤯 Kaliningrad Explained 🇪🇺",
    "description": "Russia is the biggest country on Earth 🇷🇺 But one piece of it isn't connected to the rest at all. Kaliningrad sits on the Baltic Sea, squeezed between Poland 🇵🇱 and Lithuania 🇱🇹, surrounded by NATO and the European Union 🇪🇺 For centuries it was Königsberg, founded in 1255 by the Teutonic Knights, and in 1701 the first King in Prussia was crowned there 👑 In April 1945 the Soviet army captured it, the north of East Prussia went to the USSR and the south to Poland. In 1946 the city was renamed Kaliningrad. When Lithuania became independent in 1990 and the Soviet Union dissolved in 1991, Kaliningrad was cut off 🤯 Today about one million people live there, only 65 km of Poland (the Suwałki Gap) separates it from Belarus, and Baltiysk is Russia's only Baltic port that stays ice-free in winter ⚓",
    "pinnedComment": [
      "A piece of Russia inside the EU 🇷🇺🇪🇺🤯",
      "Would you have guessed Kaliningrad used to be German Königsberg? 👑",
      "Which weird border should we explain next? 👇🗺️"
    ],
